MAKE-CONDITION on a built-in condition TYPE drops :FORMAT-CONTROL (no report).

== DONE (Slice 61) ==
Fixed with the kernel-routing approach.
  - conditions.c build_condition(): a symbol datum now routes through
    MAKE-CONDITION instead of MAKE-INSTANCE, and accepts either a kernel
    CONDITION or a CLOS instance result.  (ERROR / SIGNAL / WARN with a type
    designator all go through build_condition, so this covers all three.)
  - prelude MAKE-CONDITION wrapper: the built-in simple-condition family
    (*%kernel-condition-types* = simple-condition / simple-error / simple-warning
    / simple-type-error) routes to the kernel constructor (make_simple_condition),
    which stores FORMAT-CONTROL / FORMAT-ARGUMENTS and reports via the existing
    (PRINT-OBJECT (condition t)) method; user DEFINE-CONDITION classes still go to
    MAKE-INSTANCE with their own slots and :REPORT.
Verified 10/10: (error 'simple-error :format-control "boom ~A" :format-arguments
'(7)) reports "boom 7"; (make-condition 'simple-error :format-control "hi") princs
"hi"; simple-condition-format-control/-arguments return the stored values; the
common (error "msg ~A" 42) path still works; typep classifies the result as
simple-error / error / simple-condition; (warn 'simple-warning …) reports; and a
user (define-condition my-err (error) ((code …)) (:report …)) still builds via
make-instance with its custom slot and report intact.
Remaining nicety (not blocking): a USER condition that inherits simple-error and
passes :format-control still uses make-instance (slotless) — give the CLOS
simple-condition class real slots if that case ever matters.

== Original report ==

Creating a simple condition via a condition TYPE plus :FORMAT-CONTROL /
:FORMAT-ARGUMENTS initargs does not produce a reporting condition:

    (error 'simple-error :format-control "boom ~A" :format-arguments '(7))
       => prints "#<SIMPLE-ERROR>"   (should report "boom 7")
    (princ-to-string (make-condition 'simple-error :format-control "hi"))
       => "#<SIMPLE-ERROR>"          (should be "hi")
    (simple-condition-format-control
       (make-condition 'simple-error :format-control "boom"))
       => a #<%CLS …> object         (should be "boom")

The common format-style path is FINE:
    (handler-case (error "msg ~A" 42) (error (c) (princ-to-string c))) => "msg 42"

== Root cause ==
bocl has TWO condition representations:
  - kernel conditions (conditions.c): a CONDITION_Class object with
    format_control / format_arguments slots; make_simple_condition() builds them,
    the (PRINT-OBJECT (condition t)) method (prelude ~4703) reports them, and the
    kernel MAKE-CONDITION (conditions.c kernel_MAKE_CONDITION) already honours
    :FORMAT-CONTROL / :FORMAT-ARGUMENTS.  This is what (error "string" …) uses.
  - CLOS conditions: the standard hierarchy is ALSO registered as CLOS classes
    via ensure-clos-class (prelude ~4723) WITH NO SLOTS, so a simple-error made
    by MAKE-INSTANCE has no format-control slot and prints via the
    (PRINT-OBJECT (standard-object t)) method => "#<SIMPLE-ERROR>".

The prelude MAKE-CONDITION wrapper (prelude ~5246) routes ANY type whose
FIND-CLASS is a %cls to MAKE-INSTANCE — including the built-in simple conditions —
so the slotless CLOS path wins over the working kernel path.

== Fix sketch (conditions core — handle with care) ==
Discriminate kernel-known condition types from user DEFINE-CONDITION types in the
MAKE-CONDITION wrapper: for a built-in condition class the kernel can construct,
call the saved kernel make-condition (old-make-condition, which already does
:FORMAT-CONTROL/:FORMAT-ARGUMENTS via make_simple_condition); only fall back to
MAKE-INSTANCE for user CLOS conditions that the kernel does not know.  ERROR /
SIGNAL with a type designator route through MAKE-CONDITION, so fixing the wrapper
fixes all three.  Alternatively give the CLOS simple-condition class real
format-control/format-arguments slots + a reporting PRINT-OBJECT method, but the
kernel-routing approach reuses the already-correct kernel reporting and is less
likely to diverge.  Either way, do NOT disturb the working (error "string" …)
kernel path; verify HANDLER-CASE/SIGNAL/report all still work.

Found 2026-06-21 while regression-testing the printer work (deferred-sacla-logic-bugs);
it also explains must-printer F234/F235 erroring under (error 'simple-error …).
